<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 2.7, Essential 2.3 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-navigation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Navigation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      When an aeroplane leaves Jomo Kenyatta International Airport for Kisumu, Lagos or Dubai, the pilot must know where the destination is, how far away it is and what the local time will be on arrival. Over such distances the curve of the Earth has to be taken into account.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will locate places on the Earth's surface using latitudes and longitudes. You will calculate distances along circles of latitude and longitude in nautical miles and in kilometres, work with speed in knots and in kilometres per hour, and use the difference in longitude between two places to find their local times.
    </p>
    <p component="core">
      You will also find the course, track and drift of an aircraft or a boat that is moving through wind or a current.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pilots, ship captains at the port of Mombasa and the satellite positioning in your phone all rely on these calculations.
    </p>
  </introduction>

